Semaforismo

Ingredients: SAMBA + TRIPPY, SURREAL & PSYCHEDELIC + Alto Saxophone + 1950s ERA
Creative Catalyst: Born when dissident 1950s bohemians rewired municipal traffic lights and streetcar relays into hypnotic, polyrhythmic polygraph machines to shut down city avenues.

Semaforismo sounds like an amphetamine-fueled 1950s Rio de Janeiro street carnival colliding with an avant-garde telecommunications disaster: frenetic, syncopated samba batteries lock grooves with the mechanical chattering of high-voltage industrial relay switches, while a drenched, tape-delayed alto saxophone wails modal bebop lines that decay into dizzying, lysergic pitch-warps and tube-saturated slapback oscillations.

Genre DNA

   Tempo & Pulse: 98–114 BPM (when swaying in half-time bossa-downtilt) accelerating suddenly to 200–228 BPM in frantic, double-time street batucada; characterized by a swaggering, syncopated ginga* interrupted by hard, mechanized relay cuts and pneumatic stutters.
*   Harmonic Palette: Mid-century cool jazz chord extensions (minor 9ths, major 7#11s, altered dominants) refracted through Lydian Dominant and whole-tone scales, regularly knocked out of pitch by simulated fluctuating electrical line voltages.
*   Production Signatures: 
    1. Inductive Coil Re-amping: running live alto saxophone feeds directly through telephone carbon-capsule mics and unshielded copper inductors salvaged from municipal signal boxes to achieve biting, crackling mid-range presence.
    2. Capstan-Drag Tape Delirium: routing horn stabs through 1950s tube reel-to-reel decks where operators physically thumb the supply reel, causing woozy, sea-sick tape-head drops and wild frequency shifts.
    3. Solenoid Gating: using the acoustic clack and electrical discharges of hacked railroad relays to trigger rhythmic volume drops and mechanical transients across acoustic percussion tracks.
*   Dynamics: Tracks typically creep in on a low, menacing electrical hum punctuated by distant horn murmurs and tapping pandeiros, slowly building into an unhinged, multi-layered percussion tempest before violently collapsing into vast, dub-like analog tape hollows.

Scene & Origin Lore

In the damp Brazilian winter of 1957, following authoritarian transit curfews across downtown Rio de Janeiro, a renegade collective of favela percussionists, conservatory saxophonists, and sacked municipal switchboard operators cracked open the heavy cast-iron traffic control boxes lining Avenida Presidente Vargas. By soldering alligator clips from telephone patch bays onto electromechanical semaphore cams, they turned three-way intersections into giant, city-block-sized automated sequencers. Youth subcultures gathered in the road at 3:00 AM under blinking amber signals, wearing crisp 1950s linen suits, grease-smeared cat-eye sunglasses, and heavy rubber lineman boots to prevent electrocution while dancing on trolley tracks. The scene became a living protest ritual: whenever police sirens blared in the distance, the collective threw master knife-switches to trigger deafening streetcar alarm bells in 2/4 samba time, blowing alto saxophones into inverted sewer gratings to project hallucinatory acoustic flares across the smog-choked asphalt.

Typical Instruments
*   Lead Voice: 1950s Conn 6M "Naked Lady" or Selmer Super Balanced Action Alto Saxophone (overblown, played with virtuosic bebop phrasing, sent through carbon-element preamps and spring reverb tanks).
*   Traditional Percussion: Deep surdo, pandeiro, tamborim, cuíca, and reco-reco (scraped against metal light poles).
*   Infrastructure & Vintage Tech: Hacked Western Electric and Siemens electromechanical traffic switches, high-voltage transformer step-up coils, Ampex 350 tube tape recorders, manual patch bays, and amplified streetcar warning bells.

For Fans Of: Hermeto Pascoal, Sun Ra (1950s transition recordings), Moondog, Rahsaan Roland Kirk

