- Story & Context: Decades after state authorities welded the traffic towers shut with molten lead, elderly street sweepers swore the crunch of shattered emerald-green signal glass beneath leather shoes still rattled in 2/4 time whenever dawn broke over Praça Tiradentes. A mysterious itinerant busker known only as O Mudo climbed the rusted skeleton of the dead semaphore tower every Sunday, playing blisteringly fast alto phrases into the disused switchgear until the dead mechanical solenoids began to audibly chatter in phantom response. City engineers sent to cut down the iron structure fled in terror after their voltmeter needles spun wildly backward, picking up phantom radio broadcasts of ancient street riots humming through the cold metal armature. Scratched deep into the corroded iron base was the movement's final, lingering testament: "The green bulb is dead, but the pavement remembers the step."
